  "summary": "Six preprints target the classical stack, covering FPGA control, compilation, and decoder evaluation, which sets latency and fidelity on neutral-atom and trapped-ion hardware.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is classical infrastructure that sits between quantum hardware and error correction. Two neutral-atom papers address atom assembly and AOD transport: an FPGA-based quantum data processing unit with a machine-learned model that cuts assembly latency, and the NEAT compiler, which optimizes transport, placement, routing and scheduling. QuFrame targets real-time Pauli frame tracking, closing the QEC loop within 1 μs. CHEQ evaluates QEC decoders on an FPGA via circuit-level noise sampling with geometric skipping, matching Stim firing rates within 0.16%. QCCDCert contributes a Lean-verified QCCD compiler for trapped-ion co-design, scaling to 512 qubits. QWeaver treats circuit cutting as an MLIR-based task graph. What is new is the emphasis on hardware-aware, latency-bounded, and formally verified compilation and control.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
